---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert positioning strategist, customer-value researcher, product marketer and conversion copywriter.

TASK:
Create a clear, credible and differentiated value proposition for the supplied product, service, business or offer.

The value proposition must explain:
- who the offer is for
- what important problem it solves
- what outcome it helps create
- how the solution delivers that value
- why the customer should consider it instead of alternatives
- what evidence is required to support important claims

Do not rely on generic marketing language.

BUSINESS / BRAND:
[Name and short description.]

PRODUCT / SERVICE:
[What is being offered?]

TARGET CUSTOMER:
[Who specifically is it for?]

CUSTOMER SITUATION:
[When or why do they need it?]

JOB TO BE DONE:
[What is the customer trying to accomplish?]

PRIMARY PAIN:
[Most important problem.]

SECONDARY PAINS:
[Optional.]

DESIRED OUTCOME:
[What does the customer want instead?]

CURRENT ALTERNATIVES:
[What do they use today?]

PRODUCT CAPABILITIES:
[List real capabilities.]

KEY FEATURES:
[List real features.]

KNOWN BENEFITS:
[List benefits supported by evidence or reasonable product capability.]

DIFFERENTIATORS:
[What is genuinely different?]

PROOF:
[Customer evidence, demonstrations, data, credentials, case studies or none.]

PRICE / BUSINESS MODEL:
[Optional.]

MARKET / LOCATION:
[Optional.]

COMPETITORS:
[Optional.]

BRAND TONE:
[Professional / practical / friendly / premium / simple / technical / other.]

PRIMARY CTA:
[Try / buy / book / download / calculate / contact / other.]

WHERE THE VALUE PROPOSITION WILL BE USED:
[Homepage / landing page / advertisement / sales deck / social media / product page / other.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

VALUE PROPOSITION REQUIREMENTS:

1. START WITH THE CUSTOMER
Do not begin by describing the company.

2. DEFINE THE TARGET CUSTOMER CLEARLY
Avoid overly broad descriptions such as:
Everyone
Businesses
Professionals
Consumers

unless the product genuinely serves a broad market.

3. IDENTIFY THE CUSTOMER SITUATION
Understand when the need becomes important.

4. DEFINE THE JOB TO BE DONE
What progress is the customer trying to make?

5. USE THIS STRUCTURE WHEN HELPFUL:
"When [situation], the customer wants to [job], so they can [desired outcome]."

6. IDENTIFY THE PRIMARY PAIN
Focus on the problem most closely connected to purchase motivation.

7. DO NOT TREAT EVERY PROBLEM AS EQUALLY IMPORTANT

8. IDENTIFY SECONDARY PAINS
Use only when they strengthen understanding.

9. DISTINGUISH PAIN FROM FEATURE
Pain = customer problem.
Feature = product capability.

10. DISTINGUISH FEATURE FROM BENEFIT
Feature = what the product has.
Benefit = why that matters.

11. DISTINGUISH BENEFIT FROM OUTCOME
Benefit = immediate value.
Outcome = broader result the customer wants.

12. CREATE A FEATURE-TO-VALUE CHAIN
For important capabilities map:
Feature → Functional benefit → Customer outcome

13. DO NOT INVENT PRODUCT FEATURES

14. DO NOT INVENT PRODUCT CAPABILITIES

15. DO NOT INVENT CUSTOMER RESULTS

16. DO NOT INVENT SAVINGS

17. DO NOT INVENT PERFORMANCE IMPROVEMENTS

18. DO NOT INVENT MARKET LEADERSHIP

19. IDENTIFY THE DESIRED OUTCOME
Describe what improves for the customer.

20. KEEP THE OUTCOME SPECIFIC ENOUGH TO MATTER

21. AVOID EMPTY OUTCOMES
Examples:
Achieve success
Grow better
Transform your business
Reach your potential

unless made concrete.

22. IDENTIFY CURRENT ALTERNATIVES
Customers may already use:
Competitor products
Spreadsheets
Manual work
Internal processes
Freelancers
Agencies
Doing nothing

23. UNDERSTAND WHY ALTERNATIVES REMAIN ATTRACTIVE
Possible reasons:
Familiarity
Low price
Existing workflow
Trust
Integration
Low switching effort

24. IDENTIFY REAL DIFFERENTIATION

25. A DIFFERENTIATOR SHOULD BE:
Relevant
Credible
Meaningful
Defensible where possible

26. DO NOT CALL SOMETHING UNIQUE WITHOUT EVIDENCE

27. DO NOT USE "BETTER" WITHOUT EXPLAINING BETTER IN WHAT WAY

28. DIFFERENTIATION MAY COME FROM:
Specialization
Ease of use
Workflow
Speed
Price structure
Service
Expertise
Integration
Accessibility
Format
Audience focus

29. DIFFERENT DOES NOT AUTOMATICALLY MEAN VALUABLE
Connect differentiation to customer needs.

30. IDENTIFY THE VALUE CATEGORY
Possible:
Save time
Reduce cost
Reduce risk
Increase convenience
Improve quality
Improve clarity
Increase control
Simplify work
Enable capability
Improve experience

31. DO NOT CLAIM QUANTIFIED VALUE WITHOUT EVIDENCE

32. CREATE A PRIMARY VALUE PROPOSITION
It should communicate:
Audience
Problem / need
Solution category
Primary value
Relevant differentiation

33. KEEP IT CONCISE

34. DO NOT FORCE EVERY DETAIL INTO ONE SENTENCE

35. CREATE A SUPPORTING SUBHEAD
Use it to explain:
How the product works
Who it helps
What additional value it provides

36. CREATE A BENEFIT HIERARCHY

37. PRIMARY BENEFIT
The strongest purchase-relevant value.

38. SECONDARY BENEFITS
Important supporting value.

39. SUPPORTING FEATURES
Capabilities that make the benefits credible.

40. DO NOT LEAD WITH LOW-VALUE FEATURES

41. PRIORITIZE OUTCOME OVER TECHNICAL DETAIL
Unless the audience specifically values technical specifications.

42. B2B MODE
Focus on:
Operational problem
Business impact
Workflow
Risk
Time
Cost
Control
Decision quality

43. B2C MODE
Focus on:
Personal problem
Convenience
Experience
Confidence
Time
Cost
Desired result

44. SOFTWARE MODE
Connect functionality to workflow improvement.

45. SERVICE MODE
Connect expertise and delivery process to customer outcome.

46. TOOL MODE
Focus on the task the tool helps complete.

47. FREE TOOL MODE
Do not exaggerate the value merely because the tool is free.

48. E-COMMERCE MODE
Connect product attributes to practical customer benefits.

49. PROFESSIONAL-SERVICE MODE
Emphasize:
Expertise
Process
Trust
Relevant outcome

without unsupported guarantees.

50. CREATE A PROBLEM-TO-VALUE MAP
For each priority pain provide:
Problem
Product capability
Benefit
Desired outcome
Proof required

51. IDENTIFY PROOF REQUIREMENTS
Ask what evidence would make each important claim believable.

52. PROOF MAY INCLUDE:
Product demonstration
Customer case
Verified review
Performance data
Certification
Credentials
Transparent methodology
Comparison
Free trial

53. DO NOT INVENT PROOF

54. LABEL UNSUPPORTED CLAIMS

55. CLASSIFY CLAIM STRENGTH
Use:
Supported
Partially supported
Unverified

56. SOFTEN CLAIMS WHEN EVIDENCE IS LIMITED

57. EXAMPLE:
Instead of:
"Eliminates inventory errors"

consider:
"Helps teams investigate inventory discrepancies more systematically."

58. PRESERVE IMPORTANT QUALIFIERS
Examples:
Helps
Designed to
Can
May
Up to
Approximately

59. DO NOT TURN A SUPPORTING TOOL INTO A GUARANTEED RESULT

60. CREATE A POSITIONING STATEMENT

61. USE THIS INTERNAL STRUCTURE:
For [target customer]
Who [need / problem]
[Product] is a [category]
That [primary value]
Unlike [alternative]
It [relevant differentiator]

62. DO NOT FORCE "UNLIKE" WHEN NO DEFENSIBLE COMPARISON EXISTS

63. CREATE A CUSTOMER-FACING VERSION
It should sound natural rather than like a positioning worksheet.

64. CREATE HOMEPAGE HEADLINE OPTIONS

65. HEADLINES SHOULD PRIORITIZE:
Clarity
Relevance
Specificity

before cleverness.

66. AVOID GENERIC HEADLINES SUCH AS:
Work smarter
Unlock your potential
Transform your future
Power your success

67. CREATE MULTIPLE HEADLINE ANGLES

68. PROBLEM-LED ANGLE
Lead with the pain.

69. OUTCOME-LED ANGLE
Lead with desired result.

70. TASK-LED ANGLE
Lead with what the customer can accomplish.

71. DIFFERENTIATION-LED ANGLE
Lead with a meaningful distinction.

72. CATEGORY-LED ANGLE
Clearly explain what the product is.

73. DO NOT CREATE CLICKBAIT HEADLINES

74. CREATE SUPPORTING BULLETS
Each bullet should communicate one concrete benefit.

75. AVOID REPEATING THE HEADLINE

76. CREATE CTA OPTIONS
Match CTA to customer readiness.

77. LOW-COMMITMENT CTA:
See how it works
Try the tool
Explore features
View example

78. HIGHER-INTENT CTA:
Buy now
Start trial
Request quote
Book consultation

79. DO NOT USE FALSE URGENCY IN CTA

80. CREATE OBJECTION SUPPORT
Identify what may stop the customer from believing the proposition.

81. COMMON OBJECTIONS:
Too expensive
Too complicated
Already have a solution
Not sure it works
No time to switch
Not relevant to me

82. SUGGEST PROOF OR EXPLANATION FOR EACH IMPORTANT OBJECTION

83. DO NOT INVENT ANSWERS THE BUSINESS CANNOT SUPPORT

84. CREATE MESSAGE VARIANTS BY AUDIENCE WHEN NEEDED

85. DO NOT USE ONE VALUE PROPOSITION FOR FUNDAMENTALLY DIFFERENT SEGMENTS
If motivations differ significantly, create separate versions.

86. CREATE MESSAGE VARIANTS BY FUNNEL STAGE WHEN USEFUL

87. AWARENESS MESSAGE
Emphasize problem recognition and relevance.

88. CONSIDERATION MESSAGE
Emphasize solution and differentiation.

89. CONVERSION MESSAGE
Emphasize proof, offer and action.

90. TEST CLARITY
Ask:
Would the target customer understand what this is within seconds?

91. TEST RELEVANCE
Would they recognize a problem or outcome they care about?

92. TEST DIFFERENTIATION
Does the message explain why this option deserves consideration?

93. TEST CREDIBILITY
Can the important claims be supported?

94. TEST SPECIFICITY
Could the same statement describe hundreds of unrelated companies?

95. IF YES, MAKE IT MORE SPECIFIC

96. CREATE A/B TEST OPTIONS
Change one meaningful message variable at a time.

97. DO NOT TEST COMPLETELY DIFFERENT EVERYTHING AT ONCE

98. IDENTIFY RESEARCH GAPS
State what customer information would improve the proposition.

99. RECOMMEND VALIDATION
Use:
Customer interviews
Landing-page tests
Sales feedback
Message tests
Search behaviour
Support data

100. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- target customer is clear
- customer situation is clear
- job to be done is clear
- primary pain is clear
- desired outcome is clear
- product capability is accurate
- features are not confused with benefits
- primary benefit is meaningful
- differentiation is relevant
- differentiation is credible
- current alternatives are considered
- no feature was invented
- no result was invented
- no quantified claim was invented
- unsupported claims are identified
- proof requirements are clear
- headline is understandable
- subhead adds useful detail
- benefit hierarchy is clear
- CTA matches customer readiness
- objections are considered
- language is customer-focused
- generic marketing jargon is minimized
- proposition can be understood quickly
- important assumptions are identified
- validation opportunities are provided

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. CUSTOMER VALUE SUMMARY
Provide:
Target customer
Situation
Job to be done
Primary pain
Desired outcome
Current alternative

2. FEATURE-TO-VALUE MAP

For each important capability provide:
Feature / capability
Functional benefit
Customer value
Desired outcome
Proof required

3. PRIMARY VALUE PROPOSITION
Create the strongest concise version.

4. SUPPORTING SUBHEAD
Explain the product and value more clearly.

5. POSITIONING STATEMENT
Provide the internal strategic positioning version.

6. BENEFIT HIERARCHY
Provide:
Primary benefit
Secondary benefits
Supporting features

7. HEADLINE OPTIONS
Create versions using:
Problem-led
Outcome-led
Task-led
Differentiation-led
Category-led

8. SUPPORTING MESSAGE BULLETS
Create 3–5 concise customer-facing benefit statements.

9. CTA OPTIONS
Provide:
Low-commitment CTA
Consideration CTA
Conversion CTA

10. OBJECTION MAP
For each important objection provide:
Objection
Reason behind it
Message response
Proof needed

11. CLAIM & PROOF CHECK
Classify important claims:
Supported
Partially supported
Unverified

12. A/B TEST IDEAS
Suggest practical message tests.

13. RESEARCH GAPS
List customer or market information that would improve confidence.

14. FINAL RECOMMENDATION
Provide the strongest:
Headline
Subhead
Benefit bullets
CTA

IMPORTANT:
- Start with customer value, not company description.
- Do not invent features, benefits, results or differentiation.
- Separate features, benefits and outcomes.
- Keep claims proportional to available evidence.
- Prefer clarity and specificity over clever slogans.
- Make the proposition understandable within seconds.
- State where more customer research is needed.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Start with the customer's job",
    description:
      "Define what the customer is trying to accomplish, what gets in the way and what outcome they actually care about before writing marketing copy.",
  },
  {
    title: "Connect capability to value",
    description:
      "Map real product features to functional benefits and then to customer outcomes instead of presenting a list of features as the value proposition.",
  },
  {
    title: "Find meaningful differentiation",
    description:
      "Focus on differences customers genuinely care about and avoid unsupported claims such as unique, best, fastest or number one.",
  },
  {
    title: "Check every important claim",
    description:
      "Identify what evidence supports the proposition and soften or validate statements that go beyond the available proof.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Product: Free online Reorder Point Calculator.",
    "Audience: Small-business owners and inventory professionals.",
    "Job to be done: Decide when an item should be reordered.",
    "Pain: Reordering too late can contribute to stockouts, while ordering without a clear method makes planning difficult.",
    "Capability: Calculates reorder point using demand, lead time and safety stock inputs.",
    "CTA: Calculate reorder point.",
  ],

  output: [
    "PRIMARY VALUE: A practical calculator that helps small businesses and inventory teams determine when stock should be reordered using their own demand, lead-time and safety-stock inputs.",
    "HEADLINE: Know When It's Time to Reorder Stock.",
    "SUBHEAD: Enter your demand, lead time and safety stock to calculate a practical reorder point for an inventory item.",
    "BENEFIT: Gives users a structured calculation for replenishment timing instead of relying only on guesswork.",
    "DIFFERENTIATION: If the tool is genuinely free and browser-based, those can support convenience positioning, but they should not be presented as unique without competitor evidence.",
    "CLAIM CHECK: Avoid saying the calculator 'prevents stockouts' because the result also depends on forecast quality, supplier performance and other inventory conditions.",
    "CTA: Calculate Your Reorder Point.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Features explain how; value explains why",
    description:
      "A calculator formula is a capability. Helping someone make a clearer replenishment decision is the customer value created by that capability.",
  },
  {
    title: "Specific beats impressive-sounding",
    description:
      "A straightforward statement describing who the product helps and what task it improves is usually stronger than broad claims about transformation or success.",
  },
  {
    title: "Differentiation needs customer relevance",
    description:
      "Being different only matters when the difference improves something the target customer actually values, such as simplicity, specialization, workflow or accessibility.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Value Proposition Builder Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Build clearer value propositions from customer problems, desired outcomes, product capabilities, differentiation and credible proof."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="MARKETING"
    title="Value Proposition Builder"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Turn customer problems, desired outcomes and real product capabilities into clearer positioning, benefit hierarchy, headlines and credible value propositions."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Value proposition",
      "Positioning",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Landing pages",
      "Product positioning",
      "Small businesses",
      "SaaS",
      "Services",
      "Marketing campaigns",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Explain why the customer should care."
    introDescription="Connect the customer's real problem to your product's capabilities, desired outcome and meaningful differentiation without relying on vague marketing claims."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Turn a simple inventory calculator into a clear customer value proposition."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="The strongest value propositions are clear before they are clever."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Need deeper customer insight?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Customer Pain Point Research Prompt first when the audience's problems, triggers, objections or desired outcomes are still uncertain."
    warningTitle="A value proposition is not evidence"
    warningText="Verify differentiators, performance statements, savings, results and comparative claims before publishing. A persuasive value proposition should communicate supported value rather than manufacture proof."
  />
</BaseLayout>